<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#logothete">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Logothete</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as logothetes, logotheta, logofăt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Logothete was an administrative title of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> that rose in the middle and late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> to a senior post equivalent to a minister or secretary of state. Logothetes succeeded the fiscal officials of Late Antiquity; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> they gathered revenue in the provinces, and in the 7th century the old financial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> gave way to smaller ones, some headed by a logothete, such as the genikon for taxation and the dromos for the imperial post, diplomacy and intelligence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> created a logothete over all the departments, later the Grand Logothete. The title spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the Danubian Principalities.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tsar ruled from the palace on Tsarevets in Tarnovo with a council of great boyars and a court whose dignities, such as despot, sebastokrator, protovestiarios and <b>logothete</b>, were borrowed from Byzantine practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They served in the great bureaus under the <b>logothetes</b>, who handled the land tax, the army’s pay, the post and diplomacy, and in the themes, where tax officials assessed and collected the land tax village by village.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Three Byzantine chroniclers, Pseudo-Symeon the <b>Logothete</b>, George the Monk and Leo the Grammarian, tell that she took part in a &quot;bride show&quot; held by the Empress Dowager Euphrosyne for the young emperor Theophilos.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hymn-of-kassiani">Hymn of Kassiani</a></li><li><blockquote>Irene of Athens, sole ruler of the Byzantine Empire since 797, was overthrown by her General <b>Logothete</b>, Nikephoros, who seized the throne; she was banished to Lesbos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
